==================================================
4. IMPORTANT DATA DESIGN RULES
==================================================

CustomerNumber should be unique.

AccountNumber should be unique.

LoanNumber should be unique.

SessionId should be unique.

CreditCardId should be unique.

Do not store complete credit-card numbers.

The CreditCard entity must contain only the masked card number specified by the case study.

Do not add plaintext PIN fields.

Do not add raw PIN fields anywhere in the domain model.

Do not add unnecessary sensitive fields.

==================================================
5. PIN HASHING
==================================================

Do NOT implement the authentication service yet.

However, the database seed data must contain valid PIN hashes rather than plaintext PINs.

Use ASP.NET Core's PasswordHasher<T> for hashing demo PINs.

The raw demo PIN may exist only transiently during seed-data generation.

It must never be:

- stored in the database
- written to a source file
- written to logs
- included in application responses
- included in audit records

If PasswordHasher requires an appropriate package/reference, add it only where required.

==================================================
6. SYNTHETIC SEED DATA
==================================================

Create deterministic synthetic demonstration data for multiple customers.

Create at least:

- 3 customers
- 1 credential per customer
- multiple accounts across customers
- multiple transactions
- multiple loans
- multiple credit cards

Ensure customers have different financial records.

Include enough variation to test:

- active accounts
- dormant/closed account behavior where appropriate
- active loans
- applied loans
- closed loans
- overdue loan where appropriate
- active cards
- inactive/blocked card states where appropriate
- customer with no card
- customer with no loan

All data must be clearly synthetic.

Do not use real people's information.

Create known demo PINs for development/testing, but NEVER store the raw values in the database or source code.

If the seed mechanism requires a deterministic source PIN, isolate it so that it cannot accidentally become part of persisted data or logging.

==================================================
7. SEEDING DESIGN
==================================================

Implement seeding in a maintainable way.

The seed operation must be:

- deterministic
- idempotent
- safe to run more than once
- appropriate for development/demo environments

Do not duplicate records every time the application starts.

Do not automatically run destructive database operations.

Do not call EnsureDeleted().

Do not drop existing databases.

Do not automatically apply migrations against my Azure SQL database.

Create the migration artifacts but leave actual Azure database migration execution for a later step.

